/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A basic chopper has a DC source Vs, a controlled switch (MOSFET or IGBT with gate driver), a freewheeling diode Df, and the load.
   - Switch on: load tied to source, vo(t) = Vs.
   - The current formula assumes an R-L load with an inductor large enough to keep current continuous, or a chopper feeding an L-C filter.
   - T = Ton + Toff = 1/fs , D = Ton / T , Vo = D·Vs , Io = Vo / R = D·Vs / R
   - T is switching period in seconds; fs is switching frequency in hertz; Ton and Toff are on and off times in seconds; D is duty ratio, 0 to 1; Vs is source voltage in volts; Vo is average load voltage in volts; R is load resistance in ohms; I
   - Vo = D·Vs is an average.
   - Open loop, output obeys D·Vs and nothing corrects it; input sag or load change shifts Vo.
   - Choppers are named by what they do to voltage.
   - Two conduction modes describe inductor current.
   - In DC motor drives, choppers are also classed by the quadrants of the load’s v-i plane they work in, because a motor must send its braking energy back to the source.
*/
