/* book Worked example: Q: A chopper feeds an R-L load (R = 10 Ω, inductor large enough for continuous current) from 200 V DC. fs = 1 kHz, Ton = 0.4 ms. (a) Find D, Vo, Io and Po. (b) At 2 kHz with same Ton, find D, Vo and Po.
   1. (a) T = 1/(1×10³ Hz) = 1 ms.
   2. D = 0.4 ms / 1 ms = 0.4.
   3. Vo = D·Vs = 0.4 × 200 V = 80 V.
   4. Continuous current: Io = Vo/R = 80 V / 10 Ω = 8 A.
   5. Po = Vo·Io = 80 V × 8 A = 640 W.
   6. (b) T = 1/(2×10³ Hz) = 0.5 ms.
   7. D = 0.4 ms / 0.5 ms = 0.8.
   8. Vo = 0.8 × 200 V = 160 V; Io = 160 V / 10 Ω = 16 A; Po = 160 V × 16 A = 2560 W.
   9. Both Vo and Io doubled, so power rose four times.
   Answer: At 1 kHz: D = 0.4, Vo = 80 V, Io = 8 A, Po = 640 W. At 2 kHz: D = 0.8, Vo = 160 V, Po = 2560 W. This assumes continuous current; for a pure resistor use Po = D·Vs²/R. */
