/* book: lead: A regulated DC supply must hold its output steady when the input and the load change. A linear regulator does this by placing a transistor in series and burning the extra voltage as heat. A switched-mode power supply instead chops the input at high frequency, stores energy in an inductor or transformer, and filters the result. The first is simple and quiet but wasteful; the second reaches 70–95 %  */
