/* book Worked example: Q: A 5 V linear regulator takes 1 A from a 12 V input. (a) Find the pass-transistor dissipation and the efficiency. (b) A switched-mode supply of 85 % efficiency does the same job; find its input power, its loss, and the ratio of the linear loss to the SMPS loss. (c) The pass transistor’s junction must stay below 125 °C in 40 °C air. Find the largest junction-to-air thermal resistance allowed.
   1. Voltage across the pass device = Vin − Vo = 12 V − 5 V = 7 V.
   2. Pd = 7 V × 1 A = 7 W.
   3. ηlin = Vo / Vin = 5 / 12 = 0.4167 = 41.7 %.
   4. Po = 5 V × 1 A = 5 W.
   5. Pin = Po / 0.85 = 5 W / 0.85 = 5.88 W.
   6. Ploss = Pin − Po = 5.88 W − 5 W = 0.88 W (0.882 W before rounding).
   7. Ratio = 7 W / 0.8824 W = 7.93 (about eight times); the division uses the unrounded loss.
   8. (c) θja ≤ (Tj,max − Ta)/Pd = (125 − 40) °C / 7 W = 12.1 °C/W. A bare TO-220 package is typically 50 to 65 °C/W from junction to air, so the linear regulator needs a heat sink; the switcher’s 0.88 W does not.
   Answer: The linear regulator heats the pass device by 7 W at 41.7 % efficiency; the SMPS draws 5.88 W and loses 0.88 W, so the linear regulator's loss is 7.93 times the SMPS loss. The pass transistor needs θja ≤ 12.1 °C/W, which means a heat sink. */
